ity_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does finite additivity follow from countable additivity?","options":["Ignore the disjointness condition","Replace every finite event by the whole sample space and apply countable additivity to it","Use countable additivity with all remaining disjoint events having probability zero","Assume all events are independent"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"27e62db8244c078bd13ef2b0","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"pt_batch3_axioms_of_probability_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does monotonicity mean for nested events?","options":["A smaller event must have probability one","Nested events must be disjoint","The larger event must have probability zero whenever the smaller one is non-empty.","A smaller event has probability no larger than the event containing it"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d22b8927d547c2ac385ce7af","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch3_axioms_of_probability_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A$ and $B$ are disjoint with $P(A)=0.2$ and $P(B)=0.5$, what is $P(A\\cup B)$?","options":["$0.7$","$0.1$","$0.3$","$1.0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9787ddac353d006d4d55fa98","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch3_axioms_of_probability_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For nested die events $A=\\{1,2\\}$ and $B=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$, what should be true?","options":["$P(A)>P(B)$","$P(A)\\le P(B)$","$P(A)=1-P(B)$","$P(B)<0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bb0efeb19504ceca6ae7c523","tim